The external-device route: let it render, and let it be created honestly.

VR phase 1a (SPECS/VR_SPEC.md v3 §2.2/§4.2). Under XR_KHR_vulkan_enable2 the
OpenXR runtime creates the VkInstance and the VkDevice; Ogre is handed both as
VulkanExternalInstance / VulkanExternalDevice. Two defects at this pin make
that route unusable, and both are in code Ogre keeps to itself, so neither can
be fixed from outside the render system.

HUNK 1 — the frame veto (OgreVulkanRenderSystem.cpp).
  VulkanRenderSystem::validateDevice() returned false for ANY external device or
  instance, lost or not. Root::_fireFrameStarted() treats false as a veto on the
  frame, so an engine booted on the runtime's device renders NOTHING, every
  frame, silently. It now answers the only question it can honestly answer for a
  device it does not own: !isDeviceLost().
  Recovery stays impossible, deliberately: handleDeviceLost() (reachable ONLY
  through validateDevice) recreates the instance, the device and every resource,
  which is meaningless for a device the runtime created and whose swapchain
  images the runtime holds. A lost external device ends the XR session; the
  process restarts. Nothing on the plain path changes — mIsExternal is false
  there and the function is byte-for-byte what it was.

HUNK 2 — the device Ogre would have built, exported (OgreVulkanDevice.{h,cpp}).
  createDevice() is SKIPPED on the external path, so the ~20 device extensions
  and the five-struct VkPhysicalDeviceFeatures2 chain Ogre relies on (16-bit
  storage, shaderFloat16/int8, pipeline cache control, patch 0038's ray-query
  set, patch 0013's fifo_latest_ready) are the caller's to reproduce. Copying
  that list into our own TU would be a workaround with a shelf life — it rots
  the day a patch or an upstream bump changes the list. Instead the selection
  is EXPORTED, and the plain path is refactored to call the very same code:

    VulkanDevice::fillDeviceExtensionRequest()  — createDevice()'s extension loop
    VulkanDevice::fillDeviceFeaturesFor()       — fillDeviceFeatures()'s opt-in set
    VulkanDevice::buildFeatureChain()           — fillDeviceFeatures2()'s whole body
    VulkanDevice::buildDeviceCreationRequest()  — all three, into a caller-owned
                                                  VulkanDeviceCreationRequest whose
                                                  extensions/pNext()/features feed a
                                                  VkDeviceCreateInfo verbatim.

  createDevice(), fillDeviceFeatures() and fillDeviceFeatures2() are now thin
  callers of those, so there is ONE list and ONE chain: the internal and the
  exported device cannot drift.

  And the matching half of the same problem: on the external path
  fillDeviceFeatures2() queried the PHYSICAL DEVICE and recorded what the
  hardware SUPPORTS as though it had been ENABLED (mDeviceExtraFeatures,
  mRayQueryFeatures). shaderFloat16 + storageInputOutput16 decide
  RSC_SHADER_FLOAT16, which changes both the Hlms shader variants and the
  shader-cache fingerprint, so a device created without them still made Ogre
  compile shaders that use them. VulkanExternalDevice gains an OPTIONAL
  `creationRequest` pointer: supply the request the device was created from and
  Ogre records the enabled set from it; omit it and the old behaviour stands,
  now with a LML_CRITICAL log line saying so. Default-initialised to null, so
  every existing caller (upstream's Tutorial_VulkanExternal included) is
  unaffected.

  And three things the second read asked for (fix round 1):
  * `VulkanDeviceCreationRequest`'s copy constructor and assignment are `= delete`d.
    Its pNext chain points INTO the object, so a copy hands vkCreateDevice a chain
    that walks the original - the header's prose is now a compile error.
  * The base `VkPhysicalDeviceFeatures` is taken from the request too, not only the
    feature-chain bits: `setPhysicalDevice` had filled `mDeviceFeatures` from
    `fillDeviceFeatures()`, i.e. from what the GPU SUPPORTS, and `mSupportedStages`
    is derived from exactly two of those bits (geometry, tessellation). Today the
    two agree by construction; a caller that trims the request would otherwise
    leave Ogre believing in stages the device never enabled.
  * The external path no longer re-logs "Found device extension" for every
    extension on the device (the OpenXR caller has just enumerated and logged the
    same list to build the request), and the "hardware ray query" verdict is logged
    AFTER the override rather than only from the support query before it.

Verified: the plain (non-external) path is unchanged in behaviour — the
refactor moves code, it does not alter a single selection rule; Jahshaka's
--engine-selftest hash is byte-identical across the patch (1fd91da9...).

SOURCE patch: every tree must re-run irisgl/scripts/build-ogre.sh.
Overlaps 0002/0007/0013/0038/0040 in these files — the per-patch reverse-check
may report an "already applied" false positive; judge by content and reset the
submodule before build-ogre.sh.
